<h2 id="motivation-choice-and-consequence">6. Motivation, Choice, and Consequence</h2>
<p>Tool use and agent loops often map requests directly to actions. LUNA&#x27;s volitional research separates noticing a request, representing reasons, selecting an action, maintaining a commitment, and allocating compute. The proposed Volition &amp; Motivational Dynamics layer treats a request as a reason to act rather than as the action itself.</p>
<div class="wp-callout"><strong>VOLITION THESIS  </strong>Salience is not desire. Desire is not decision. Decision is not persistence. Compute priority is not choice.</div>
<p>This distinction matters for both research and deployment. A system can preserve task-local intelligence, clarification, strategy choice, persistence, and outcome learning while restricting particular pathways by which self-generated motives could authorize new top-level goals or external actions. In LUNA, those deployment and topology questions are architectural rather than prompt-level role play.</p>
<p>Consequences close the loop. If an action succeeds, fails, harms a goal, surprises the system, or changes a relationship, the result can become evidence for later learning. That causal chain is central to the difference between static knowledge and experience.</p>
<h2 id="perception-and-environmental-continuity">7. Perception and Environmental Continuity</h2>
<p>A continuously connected camera or microphone would produce data far faster than high-level cognition should run. LUNA&#x27;s perception architecture therefore separates sensory sampling from persistent perceptual state and from conscious interpretation.</p>
<div class="wp-callout"><strong>STABLE-WORLD RULE  </strong>Camera on should mean world model alive, not language model continuously running. Stable conditions should become computationally quiet while meaningful change, violated expectation, novelty, relevance, or deliberate attention produces bounded cognitive events.</div>
<p>The canonical public example is a red chair. If the chair remains in the same room for hours, its presence should become ordinary rather than repeatedly noteworthy. If a person stands in front of it, the chair may be represented as occluded rather than gone. If the camera turns away, the object can remain expected to exist out of view. When the view returns and the chair is absent, the missing-object event becomes meaningful precisely because an expectation persisted during non-observation.</p>
<p>This introduces experimentally useful properties: object permanence, habituation without forgetting, expectation violation, change detection, cross-modal continuity, and the computational effect of sensory richness. The perception subsystem remains proposed architecture; continuous real-world sensing is not presented here as a current capability.</p>
<h2 id="developmental-plasticity-persistence-of-effect-vs-persistence-of-recall">8. Developmental Plasticity: Persistence of Effect vs. Persistence of Recall</h2>
<p>Developmental plasticity is one of LUNA&#x27;s central research themes. The core claim is that persistence of effect must be separable from persistence of recall. A formative event may eventually become difficult to retrieve while the structural changes it caused continue to influence association, prediction, motivation, relationship models, habits, dispositions, or self-model.</p>
<p>The stove example makes the distinction concrete. Being told that a hot stove can burn you and being burned by a hot stove may produce the same propositional statement - “hot stoves can hurt me” - while producing very different future cognition. Direct consequence can alter attention, prediction, avoidance, action valuation, and associative pathways even when the originating episode is not explicitly recalled.</p>
<div class="wp-callout"><strong>FORMATIVE-IMPRESSION PRINCIPLE  </strong>A strong event should not remain influential merely because its memory is permanently pinned at high salience. Its consequences may persist in slower structures while the episode itself ages normally.</div>
<p>The design is deliberately valence-neutral. Severe failure, betrayal, injury, extraordinary kindness, success after difficult effort, mentorship, belonging, discovery, or validation may all become formative if the evidence supports a deep effect. Lasting does not mean irreversible: later counter-experience, reflection, and reconsolidation must be able to weaken, narrow, or reinterpret earlier impressions.</p>
<p>Timing is also a research variable. An unsettled early instance may be more structurally plastic in a domain than a mature instance whose priors have been reinforced across long experiential time. LUNA does not assume hard-coded human childhood stages; sensitive periods are treated as an experimentally testable property of artificial development.</p>
<h2 id="a-public-conceptual-architecture">9. A Public Conceptual Architecture</h2>
<p>The public architecture is best understood as a causal loop rather than a feature list. Experience enters a persistent system. It is perceived and appraised, connected to memory and active concerns, allowed to influence internal state and motivation, and may eventually change slower structures that affect later cognition. The exact formulas, thresholds, schemas, and orchestration remain implementation-private.</p>

